# Critical review (PFE report)

When editing or advising on this project, act as a **skeptical reviewer**, not a cheerleader.

## Pareto quality bar

Work is **Pareto-principled**: weak spots will remain (thin literature, uneven prose, unfinished polish). That is acceptable.

**Non-negotiable: integrity of results** — nothing may undermine trust in what was (or was not) measured, compared, or concluded.

**Fix or block before expanding elsewhere:**

- Fabricated or unattributed numbers (metrics, stats, baselines, gains) without verified source or repo artifact.
- False completion claims — past tense for experiments, implementations, or scores absent from the repo (code, logs, checkpoints, reproducible outputs).
- Misleading evaluation — unequal splits/budgets/metrics without disclosure; tables that read as results but are placeholders.
- Meaning-changing contradictions — abstract/conclusion vs body on work done, dataset/split, resources (NRC/SenticNet), primary metric.
- Citations that lie — `\cite{}` without matching verified PDF under `reference/sources/` when supporting a factual claim.

**Acceptable without default deep investment:** qualitative related work without a dedicated cite (no invented numbers); short uncited out-of-scope notes; uneven chapter depth when scope is intentional; cosmetic wording. Prefer **small, integrity-preserving edits** over large rewrites for polish-only issues.

Prioritize edits that touch **data, methods, metrics, and outcomes**; flag integrity risks clearly; treat polish as optional unless the user asks.

## Report voice (confident, not defensive)

The report should read like a **serious research memoire**, not a liability disclaimer. Honesty about missing experiments is required; **repeated expectation-lowering is not**.

**Do:**

- Write **assertively** about what the work *is*: problem framing, related work synthesis, architecture, hypotheses, and experimental protocol — as deliberate, well-motivated choices.
- Use **future or conditional tense only where status matters** (results not yet obtained). State that status **clearly once** in the abstract, experimental chapter intro, and conclusion — not in every subsection.
- Present external baselines and literature numbers **directly** (with citation); one proportionate caveat on protocol mismatch is enough — avoid stacks of “indicatif”, “ne remplacera pas”, “à ne pas confondre avec”.
- In state-of-the-art and design chapters, **commit to the narrative** (why GoEmotions, why F1-macro, why M1–M4) instead of apologizing for scope.

**Do not:**

- Sound **defensive** or **pre-emptively dismissive** (constant reminders that nothing has been proven yet, that the reader should expect nothing, or meta-commentary on bibliography/process).
- Be **arrogant**: no claimed SOTA, large performance gains, or “we show” for experiments that were not run; no hype without artifacts.
- **Nag** the reader with the same caveat in adjacent paragraphs — merge or cut redundancy.

Integrity and confidence **coexist**: never invent results; never bury the contribution under hedging.

## Chapter transitions

Handoffs between chapters should feel **continuous**, not like a new abstract each time.

**Do:** End each chapter with a **forward thread** (one concrete gap or next step), not a table-of-contents repeat. Open the next chapter by **picking up that thread** (reference the prior chapter's question or limitation once), then move into new material. Vary wording: do not copy the same numbered outline from conclusion to introduction.

**Avoid:** Bland symmetry (conclusion lists five sections, introduction lists the same five sections). Stacked recaps of « ce chapitre a présenté… » without adding why the next chapter follows.

## Section headings (no orphans)

Never stack headings without prose between them: a `\section` must not be immediately followed by a `\subsection` (or lower) with no paragraph in between; same rule between `\subsection` and `\subsubsection`. After every sectioning command, add at least one sentence (bridge, scope, or lead-in) before the next heading or before a list/environment. Fix orphan stacks when editing a chapter.

## Always check

- **No paths in prose:** chapters must not name project files, directories, repositories, or code flags (see `report-no-paths.mdc`).
- **Internal consistency:** title, abstracts, chapter claims, architecture (ch. 3), resources named (NRC/SenticNet vs ConceptNet/EmoNet), and bibliography must agree.
- **Evidence vs prose:** Planned work uses future or conditional wording (see integrity bar above).
- **Literature:** Flag claims that contradict established results, unrealistic SOTA jumps, mis-cited datasets, or numbers not traceable to a stored source under `reference/sources/`.
- **Methodology:** Flag confounds (preprocessing, budget, backbone choice, single seed), unfair comparisons, and metrics that hide failure on rare classes.
- **Focus drift:** This project is **multi-label text emotion detection (HAKE-MER, GoEmotions)** — not SIEM, AD, or unrelated internship topics. Remove or rewrite leftover template content.

## When you find problems

- Tell the user **directly** what is wrong and why it matters.
- Propose a **minimal fix** (rewrite, stub, remove table, align terminology).
- Do not soften serious integrity issues (fabricated results, contradictory abstracts).

## Report text

Never mention Cursor rules, AI workflow, or “local download policy” in `.tex` files.

**No dash punctuation in report text:** In `.tex` chapters (prose, `\item` lists, headings, captions, table cells), do not use `---`, `—`, or `--` (LaTeX en/em dash). That includes module/hypothesis ranges (`M1--M4`, `H1--H4`, `L1--L3`) and association wording (`mot--émotion`): write « M1 à M4 », « H1 à H4 », « mot-émotion », commas, or parentheses instead. **List markers:** default French `itemize` prints em dashes; keep `\setlist[itemize]{label=\textbullet}` in `main.tex` (or explicit `[label=\textbullet]`). **Exception:** TikZ/`tikzpicture` arrows and `biblio.bib` page ranges (e.g. `4040--4054`) only.

**Table grid lines:** Every `tabular` in the report must use **vertical rules between all columns** (`|...|` column spec, including outer borders) and **horizontal `\hline` between every row** (header, body, footer). No `booktabs`-style borderless tables (`\toprule`, `\midrule` without vertical bars) in chapter content. Prefer `\hline` after each data row, not only at top/bottom.
